  "text": "Rivers shape valleys through the steady movement of water across the landscape. As a river flows downhill, it erodes soil and rock, cutting channels that gradually deepen and widen. In its upper course, fast-moving water often carves narrow, steep-sided V-shaped valleys, especially where the land is hard and resistant. As the river reaches flatter ground, its speed decreases, allowing it to meander from side to side. These bends erode their outer banks while depositing sediment along inner banks, gradually creating broader, flatter valleys. During floods, rivers spread layers of fertile silt across surrounding land, forming floodplains that support plants, animals, and human communities. Tributaries add water and erosion, helping expand the valley system. Over thousands or millions of years, this combination of erosion, transportation, and deposition transforms rugged terrain into distinctive landscapes. Rivers therefore act as both sculptors and builders, continuously reshaping valleys while carrying fragments of mountains toward lakes and seas.",
